\subsection{Constitutive Formulations}

\paragraph{Hydraulic Conductance ($K$) and Resistance ($R$):}
Liquid-phase conductance defines the ratio of flow to the hydrostatic/water-potential driving gradient:
\begin{equation}
    J = K \cdot \Delta\Psi, \qquad R = \frac{1}{K}
\end{equation}
In molar units per unit leaf surface area, an area-specific conductance $K_L$ carries units of $\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}\,MPa^{-1}}$. Multiplying by molar mass $M_w = 1.8015 \times 10^{-2}\,\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$ translates the flux directly into mass flux density ($\mathrm{kg\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$).

\paragraph{Hydraulic Capacitance ($C$):}
Capacitance quantifies the change in tissue water storage volume $V$ (or molar content $W$) per unit change in water potential:
\begin{equation}
    C = \frac{\mathrm{d}V}{\mathrm{d}\Psi} \quad \left[\mathrm{m^3\,MPa^{-1}}\right], \qquad C_L = \frac{\mathrm{d}W}{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L} \quad \left[\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,MPa^{-1}}\right]
\end{equation}
The dimensional product of resistance and capacitance yields the characteristic relaxation time of the tissue:
\begin{equation}
    [\tau] = [R] \cdot [C] = \left(\frac{\mathrm{MPa}}{\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}}}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}}}{\mathrm{MPa}}\right) = \mathrm{s}
\end{equation}

