\section{Derivation I: Steady-State Flow Network}

Consider a multi-compartment representation of the Soil-Plant-Atmosphere Continuum consisting of four discrete nodes connected in series:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Bulk Soil ($\Psi_{\mathrm{soil}}$)
    \item Root Xylem ($\Psi_{\mathrm{root}}$)
    \item Stem / Petiole Xylem ($\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}$)
    \item Sub-stomatal Leaf Mesophyll ($\Psi_L$)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Resistive Chain Equations}

Under the steady-state assumption, the net storage change in all internal nodes is identically zero:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}V_{\mathrm{root}}}{\mathrm{d}t} = \frac{\mathrm{d}V_{\mathrm{stem}}}{\mathrm{d}t} = \frac{\mathrm{d}V_{\mathrm{leaf}}}{\mathrm{d}t} = 0
\end{equation}
Consequently, the mass flux density through each compartment is spatially uniform and equals the transpiration rate $E$:
\begin{align}
    E &= K_{\mathrm{soil-root}}\left(\Psi_{\mathrm{soil}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{root}}\right) \\
    E &= K_{\mathrm{root-stem}}\left(\Psi_{\mathrm{root}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}\right) \\
    E &= K_{\mathrm{stem-leaf}}\left(\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - \Psi_L\right)
\end{align}

Expressing these relations in terms of hydraulic resistances $R_i = K_i^{-1}$:
\begin{align}
    \Psi_{\mathrm{soil}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{root}} &= E \cdot R_{\mathrm{soil-root}} \\
    \Psi_{\mathrm{root}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} &= E \cdot R_{\mathrm{root-stem}} \\
    \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - \Psi_L &= E \cdot R_{\mathrm{stem-leaf}}
\end{align}

Summing the entire potential drop from soil to leaf apoplast:
\begin{equation}
    \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{soil}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{root}}\right) + \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{root}} - \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}\right) + \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - \Psi_L\right) = E \left(R_{\mathrm{soil-root}} + R_{\mathrm{root-stem}} + R_{\mathrm{stem-leaf}}\right)
\end{equation}
Defining total liquid-phase plant resistance as $R_{\mathrm{plant}} = \sum R_i$:
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_{\mathrm{soil}} - \Psi_L = E \cdot R_{\mathrm{plant}} = \frac{E}{K_{\mathrm{plant}}}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Coupling to Atmospheric Vapor Flux}

Transpiration across the leaf cuticle and stomatal pores into the turbulent atmospheric boundary layer is driven by the mole fraction vapor gradient:
\begin{equation}
    E = g_{\mathrm{tot,v}} \cdot \left(\frac{e_s(T_L) - e_a}{P_{\mathrm{atm}}}\right) = g_{\mathrm{tot,v}} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{VPD}_L}{P_{\mathrm{atm}}}
\end{equation}
where $g_{\mathrm{tot,v}} = \left(g_{\mathrm{stomatal}}^{-1} + g_{\mathrm{boundary}}^{-1}\right)^{-1}$ is total vapor conductance ($\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$), $e_s(T_L)$ is saturation vapor pressure at leaf temperature, and $e_a$ is atmospheric vapor pressure.

Equating liquid supply with vapor demand yields the steady-state operating leaf water potential:
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_L^{\mathrm{ss}} = \Psi_{\mathrm{soil}} - \frac{g_{\mathrm{tot,v}} \cdot \mathrm{VPD}_L}{P_{\mathrm{atm}} \cdot K_{\mathrm{plant}}}
\end{equation}
Equation (15) demonstrates that at steady state, leaf water potential becomes increasingly negative linearly with both increasing vapor pressure deficit and declining whole-plant conductance.

\section{Derivation II: Transient Leaf Water-Balance Model}

Under dynamic environmental conditions (e.g., fluctuating irradiance, wind gusts, rapid stomatal closure), the rate of leaf water loss exceeds xylem recharge, engaging internal tissue storage.

\subsection{Conservation of Mass with Capacitive Storage}

Let $W_L(t)$ be the area-normalized water content of the leaf ($\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}}$). By the principle of continuity:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}W_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} = J_{\mathrm{in}}(t) - E(t)
\end{equation}
where $J_{\mathrm{in}}(t)$ is the incoming xylem flux from the stem, and $E(t)$ is the instantaneous evaporative loss.

Applying the chain rule, the rate of change of water content is linked to leaf water potential via capacitance $C_L$:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}W_L}{\mathrm{d}t} = \left(\frac{\mathrm{d}W_L}{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L}\right) \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} = C_L \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t}
\end{equation}

The xylem supply flux is driven by the potential gradient between the stem supply reservoir $\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}$ and the leaf $\Psi_L(t)$ across xylem resistance $R_x = K_x^{-1}$:
\begin{equation}
    J_{\mathrm{in}}(t) = K_x \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}(t) - \Psi_L(t)\right)
\end{equation}

Substituting Equations (17) and (18) into Equation (16):
\begin{equation}
    C_L \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} = K_x \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}(t) - \Psi_L(t)\right) - E(t)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Standard Form of the Governing ODE}

Dividing by $K_x$ and rearranging into standard first-order non-homogeneous form:
\begin{equation}
    \tau \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} + \Psi_L(t) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}(t) - R_x E(t)
\end{equation}
where the characteristic hydraulic time constant $\tau$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \tau \equiv R_x C_L = \frac{C_L}{K_x}
\end{equation}
For typical woody angiosperms, with $C_L \approx 500\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,MPa^{-1}}$ and $K_x \approx 5\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}\,MPa^{-1}} = 0.005\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}\,MPa^{-1}}$:
\begin{equation}
    \tau \approx \frac{500}{0.005} = 100\,000\,\mathrm{s} \approx 27.7\,\mathrm{h} \quad (\text{whole plant}),
\end{equation}
whereas for an isolated foliar blade with $C_L \approx 100\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,MPa^{-1}}$ and localized mesophyll pathway $K_x \approx 0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}\,MPa^{-1}}$, $\tau$ ranges between $10$ to $60\,\mathrm{minutes}$.

\section{Analytical Dynamics and Response Regimes}

\subsection{Case 1: Step Change in Transpiration (Indicial Response)}

Consider a leaf initially in static equilibrium with stem supply at $t=0^-$ ($\Psi_L(0) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}$). At $t = 0$, stomata open instantaneously to a constant transpiration demand $E(t) = E_0$:
\begin{equation}
    \tau \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} + \Psi_L(t) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x E_0, \qquad \Psi_L(0) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}
\end{equation}
Using the integrating factor $\mu(t) = e^{t/\tau}$:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\left(\Psi_L(t) e^{t/\tau}\right) = \frac{1}{\tau} \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x E_0\right) e^{t/\tau}
\end{equation}
Integrating from $0$ to $t$:
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_L(t) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x E_0 \left(1 - e^{-t/\tau}\right)
\end{equation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item At $t \ll \tau$: $\Psi_L(t) \approx \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - \frac{E_0}{C_L} t$; the rate of water loss is supplied almost entirely by depleting internal leaf capacitance.
    \item At $t \to \infty$: $\Psi_L(t) \to \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x E_0 = \Psi_L^{\mathrm{ss}}$; storage flux ceases, and xylem inflow matches transpiration.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Case 2: Periodic Diurnal Forcing and Hydraulic Hysteresis}

Under natural daylight cycles, solar radiation and vapor pressure deficit induce approximately sinusoidal transpiration forcing:
\begin{equation}
    E(t) = \bar{E} + E_a \cos(\omega t)
\end{equation}
where $\omega = \frac{2\pi}{86400}\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}} \approx 7.27 \times 10^{-5}\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$.

Assuming constant $\Psi_{\mathrm{stem}}$, Equation (20) becomes:
\begin{equation}
    \tau \frac{\mathrm{d}\Psi_L(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} + \Psi_L(t) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x \bar{E} - R_x E_a \cos(\omega t)
\end{equation}
The particular periodic solution is obtained via harmonic balance:
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_L(t) = \Psi_{\mathrm{stem}} - R_x \bar{E} - \frac{R_x E_a}{\sqrt{1 + (\omega \tau)^2}} \cos\left(\omega t - \phi\right)
\end{equation}
where the phase lag $\phi$ between peak transpiration and maximum leaf tension is:
\begin{equation}
    \phi = \arctan(\omega \tau)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Physical Consequence:} Because $\phi > 0$, peak transpiration occurs before minimum (most negative) leaf water potential is reached. When plotting $E(t)$ against $\Psi_L(t)$ over a 24-hour cycle, this phase lag generates an elliptical trajectory known as \textbf{diurnal hydraulic hysteresis}. Capacitance buffers the leaf against abrupt mid-morning tension spikes at the expense of delayed late-afternoon rehydration.

